		{"id":"d82a69cf4be61169","type":"text","text":"Topics:\n* Tessellation\n\t* Voronoi\n\t* Delaunay (dual Voronoi)\n* Tiling\n* Space filling\n* Space partitioning\n* Packing","x":-1280,"y":-169,"width":554,"height":341},
		{"id":"d6048ef572a4f622","type":"text","text":"# Procedural Modeling: Tessellations","x":-2000,"y":-71,"width":540,"height":72},
		{"id":"6f69b99cc5275fd0","type":"text","text":"A tessellation, or tiling, is a **division** of the plane (space) into figures (cells) called tiles using certain rules and constraints.\n\t\t\t\t\tVS\nA tessellation or tiling is the **covering** of a surface, often a plane, using one or more geometric shapes, called tiles, using certain rules and constraints.","x":620,"y":-600,"width":560,"height":245},
		{"id":"440a02a9ed781f8b","type":"text","text":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2vfQ_df9PMY","x":4440,"y":922,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"fafdf06b926a462e","x":3975,"y":1064,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=91qscdiIVsU"},
		{"id":"78eba1ec72165a55","type":"text","text":"Tools for creating tessellations","x":4992,"y":-480,"width":337,"height":80},
		{"id":"f3ac2da5f3640ad4","x":6607,"y":646,"width":400,"height":102,"type":"file","file":"Images/Tessellation/Tools/NonEuclideanGeo.JPG"},
		{"id":"410afc04c547e307","x":6607,"y":518,"width":320,"height":104,"type":"text","text":"The essential difference between the metric geometries is the nature of parallel lines."},
		{"id":"276ae2bcfdddc4d6","x":6607,"y":748,"width":250,"height":60,"type":"text","text":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-Euclidean_geometry"},
		{"id":"05737f09881bbce6","type":"text","text":"https://docs.blender.org/manual/en/4.1/addons/mesh/tissue.html","x":4960,"y":1520,"width":400,"height":73},
